\addlinespace

\textbf{Primary Outcome 2: Reemergence} & Time-to-event (months): Time from disease-free period ($\geq$2 years) to next outbreak & \textbf{$\geq$2 years:} Distinguishes reintroduction from persistence. \textbf{Time-to-event:} Captures temporal dynamics, handles right-censoring. \textbf{Survival analysis:} Standard method for time-to-event data. \\
\addlinespace

% EXPOSURE/PREDICTORS
\textbf{Predictor Categories} & (1) Ecological, (2) Environmental, (3) Wildlife, (4) Infrastructure, (5) Institutional & \textbf{Multi-factor:} Disease recurrence driven by multiple pathways. \textbf{Testable:} Each category has biological mechanism. \textbf{Actionable:} Several modifiable through policy (infrastructure, institutional). \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Primary Predictors} & Livestock density, tree cover, road density, wildlife proximity, vaccination/culling coverage, GDP & \textbf{Evidence-based:} Prior ASF literature supports these. \textbf{Data availability:} Exist for all countries/diseases. \textbf{Mechanistic:} Clear pathways to transmission. \\
\addlinespace

% UNIT OF ANALYSIS
\textbf{Unit of Analysis} & Admin-2 (commune/county level) & \textbf{Data constraint:} WAHIS reports outbreaks at admin-2. \textbf{Meaningful:} Matches veterinary control units. \textbf{Actionable:} Policies implemented at admin-2. \textbf{Balance:} Fine enough for spatial variation, coarse enough for adequate sample size. \\
\addlinespace

% SPATIAL SCALE
\textbf{Spatial Scale Justification} & Why not farm-level? Why not admin-1? & \textbf{Not farm:} WAHIS doesn't provide exact locations (privacy). \textbf{Not admin-1:} Too coarse, misses within-province variation. \textbf{Admin-2 optimal:} Best balance between resolution and data availability. \\
\addlinespace

% STUDY LOCATION
\textbf{Study Location} & (1) Pooled analysis: 15 TADs across high-burden countries. (2) Case study: Romania (ASF) & \textbf{Pooled:} Tests generalizability, identifies common risk factors. \textbf{Case study:} Deep dive into single disease-country for detailed mechanisms. \textbf{Romania:} Highest ASF burden (66K outbreaks), excellent data, represents Europe. \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Why Romania?} & See Section 3 & (1) Data availability: Complete outbreak records, farm registry, spatial data. (2) High burden: 66,530 ASF outbreaks (2017-2024). (3) Epidemic characteristics: Sustained transmission, diverse landscapes, commercial + backyard farms. (4) Generalizability: European context with global ASF relevance. \\
\addlinespace

% STUDY PERIOD
\textbf{Study Period (Pooled)} & 2005-2025 (20 years) & \textbf{WAHIS coverage:} Comprehensive global TAD reporting since 2005. \textbf{Long enough:} Captures multiple epidemic waves, persistence/reemergence cycles. \textbf{Recent enough:} Reflects current agricultural systems, control capacity. \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Study Period (Romania)} & 2018-2024 (6 years) & \textbf{Post-introduction:} ASF entered Romania 2017, established transmission 2018+. \textbf{Sufficient outbreaks:} Adequate sample for modeling. \textbf{Data quality:} Modern surveillance, complete records. See Section 4 for full justification. \\
\addlinespace

% SAMPLE SIZE
\textbf{Sample Size (Romania)} & 1,976 ASF outbreaks across 537 admin-2 units & \textbf{Adequate power:} 404 events per variable (far exceeds 10 EPV rule). \textbf{Spatial coverage:} Outbreaks in 537 of 3,181 communes (16.9\%). \textbf{Temporal coverage:} 6 years allows persistence and reemergence analysis. \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Events Per Variable (EPV)} & 404 EPV (1,976 outbreaks / 5 predictors) & \textbf{Exceeds threshold:} Standard requires 10-20 EPV. We have 404 EPV = 40× higher. \textbf{Implication:} Extremely stable coefficient estimates, minimal overfitting risk, robust predictions. \\
\addlinespace

% DATA SOURCES
\textbf{Primary Data Source} & WAHIS (WOAH World Animal Health Information System) & \textbf{Global standard:} Official OIE/WOAH outbreak reporting system. \textbf{Standardized:} Consistent definitions across countries/diseases. \textbf{Validated:} National veterinary authorities verify reports. \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Livestock Data} & GLW (Gridded Livestock of the World), FAOSTAT & \textbf{GLW:} High-resolution (10km) livestock density grids. \textbf{FAOSTAT:} National production statistics. \textbf{Combined:} Captures spatial variation in susceptible populations. \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Environmental Data} & MODIS (tree cover), WorldClim (climate), HydroSHEDS (water) & \textbf{Remote sensing:} Objective, spatially explicit, temporally consistent. \textbf{Resolution:} 30m-1km matches admin-2 scale. \textbf{Validated:} Standard products used globally. \\
\addlinespace

% STATISTICAL METHODS
\textbf{Model 1: Persistence} & Mixed-effects logistic regression & \textbf{Binary outcome:} Logistic regression standard for yes/no. \textbf{Random effects:} Accounts for clustering (disease $\rightarrow$ country $\rightarrow$ admin-2). \textbf{Interpretable:} Odds ratios directly policy-relevant. \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Model 2: Reemergence} & Cox proportional hazards with frailty & \textbf{Time-to-event:} Cox standard for survival analysis. \textbf{Frailty:} Random effects for clustering. \textbf{Handles censoring:} Units without reemergence not lost. \textbf{Interpretable:} Hazard ratios show timing effects. \\
\addlinespace

% VARIABLE SELECTION
\textbf{Variable Selection} & (1) Univariate screening, (2) Expert knowledge, (3) Correlation analysis & \textbf{Univariate:} Identifies significant predictors (p<0.25). \textbf{Expert:} Includes biological plausibility. \textbf{Correlation:} Removes redundancy (r>0.7 excluded). \textbf{Combined:} Balances statistics with mechanism. \\
\addlinespace

% MODEL VALIDATION
\textbf{Train/Test Split} & 70\% training, 30\% testing (stratified by outcome) & \textbf{Standard:} 70/30 widely used in epidemiology. \textbf{Stratified:} Ensures balanced outcome distribution. \textbf{Independent test:} Assesses generalizability. \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Cross-Validation} & 10-fold within training set & \textbf{Detects overfitting:} Training vs CV performance gap indicates overfitting. \textbf{10-fold:} Balance between bias and variance. \textbf{Repeated:} Average across folds for stability. \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Performance Metrics} & AUC (ROC), calibration, sensitivity, specificity & \textbf{AUC:} Overall discrimination (0.7+ good, 0.8+ excellent). \textbf{Calibration:} Predicted vs observed match. \textbf{Sens/Spec:} Policy-relevant (miss high-risk vs false alarms). \\
\addlinespace

% DIAGNOSTICS
\textbf{Multicollinearity} & VIF <5 & \textbf{VIF:} Variance Inflation Factor detects collinearity. \textbf{Threshold:} VIF>5 problematic, VIF>10 severe. \textbf{Action:} Remove correlated predictors (r>0.7). \\
\addlinespace

\textbf{Spatial Autocorrelation} & Moran's I on residuals & \textbf{Detects clustering:} Are residuals spatially structured? \textbf{Interpretation:} Significant Moran's I = missing spatial predictors. \textbf{Action:} Add spatial terms or interpret as unmeasured processes. \\
\addlinespace

% ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS
\textbf{Ethics} & No IRB required (secondary data, no human subjects) & \textbf{Data sources:} All publicly available (WAHIS, GLW, FAOSTAT). \textbf{No PII:} Outbreak data aggregated to admin-2, no individual farms/farmers identified. \textbf{Animal welfare:} Study aims to reduce outbreaks = fewer cullings. \\
\addlinespace

% REPRODUCIBILITY
\textbf{Reproducibility} & All code on GitHub, data sources documented, analysis scripted & \textbf{Open science:} R scripts + data links = fully reproducible. \textbf{Extensibility:} Framework applicable to other diseases/countries. \textbf{Transparency:} Methods section provides exact steps. \\
\addlinespace

% LIMITATIONS
\textbf{Key Limitations} & (1) Observational (no causality), (2) Reporting bias, (3) Ecological fallacy & \textbf{Observational:} Can't establish causation, only association. \textbf{Reporting:} Underreporting biases estimates (but assumes consistent underreporting). \textbf{Ecological:} Admin-2 associations may not hold at farm level. \\
\addlinespace

\end{longtable}

